::ALG2_U02_L06_GP01::
Question ID: ALG2_U02_L06_GP01
MLA Standard: Support-only application; no new primary MLA standard
Question:
If x represents a length, which value is not viable?
{
=A. x = -4 #Correct. Lengths cannot be negative.
~B. x = 0 #Zero may be possible depending on the context.
~C. x = 3 #Positive lengths can be viable.
~D. x = 12 #Positive lengths can be viable.
}

::ALG2_U02_L06_GP02::
Question ID: ALG2_U02_L06_GP02
MLA Standard: Support-only application; no new primary MLA standard
Question:
In h(t) = -16t(t - 5), what does t = 5 usually represent?
{
~A. initial height #Initial height is h(0).
~B. maximum height #Maximum height occurs at the vertex.
=C. landing time #Correct. The later zero is when height returns to 0.
~D. starting time #Starting time is t = 0.
}

::ALG2_U02_L06_GP03::
Question ID: ALG2_U02_L06_GP03
MLA Standard: Support-only application; no new primary MLA standard
Question:
What should be checked after solving a polynomial model?
{
~A. only the leading coefficient #The leading coefficient is not enough.
=B. whether each solution is reasonable in context #Correct. Context decides viable solutions.
~C. only the degree #The degree is not enough.
~D. only the constant term #The constant term is not enough.
}

::ALG2_U02_L06_GP04::
Question ID: ALG2_U02_L06_GP04
MLA Standard: Support-only application; no new primary MLA standard
Question:
If P(x) models profit, what does P(x) = 0 represent?
{
~A. maximum loss #A maximum loss would be a minimum value.
~B. domain restriction #A domain restriction limits inputs.
~C. negative profit #Negative profit is below zero.
=D. break-even point #Correct. Profit of 0 is break-even.
}

::ALG2_U02_L06_GP05::
Question ID: ALG2_U02_L06_GP05
MLA Standard: Support-only application; no new primary MLA standard
Question:
Why might a complex solution be rejected in a physical length model?
{
~A. complex solutions are always wrong #Complex solutions may be valid algebraically.
=B. physical lengths must be real numbers #Correct. Physical lengths are real-valued measurements.
~C. all negative values are complex #Negative real values are not complex.
~D. models never have complex values #Some equations can have complex solutions.
}

